    "lawCode": "Amd §399-z, Gen Bus L",
    "lawSection": "General Business Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S6034\n\n TITLE OF BILL :\n\nAn act to amend the general business law, in relation to providing for\nenhanced consumer protection measures and enforcement of the\ndo-not-call registry\n\n\n PURPOSE :\n\nThis bill amends the do-not-call law to create more comprehensive\nprotections from unsolicited telemarketing sales calls for consumers.\nThe bill expressly prohibits pre-recorded telemarketing sales calls;\nrestricts telemarketer calling times; requires telemarketers to\ndisclose all terms and conditions related to offers containing\nnegative option features; and provides the Consumer Protection Board\n(CPB) with subpoena power to investigate violations.\n\n SUMMARY OF PROVISIONS :\n\nSection 1 of the bill amends General Business Law (GBL) § 399-z to do\nthe following:\n\n* define the term \"negative option feature.\"\n\n* include within the definition of \"telemarketing sales call\" any\noutbound telephone calling technology that delivers a prerecorded\nunsolicited telemarketing message to a customer or to his or her\nvoicemail or answering machine service.\n\n* limit the time of day that a telemarketer may call and requires a\ntelemarketer to provide consumers with its name, the purpose of the\ncall, and the identity and cost of the goods or services being\noffered, and the terms and conditions related to any offers that\ninclude a negative option.\n\n* provide the CPB with subpoena power during the investigatory phase\nof its enforcement proceedings.\n\n* clarify the elements to be established for an alleged violator to\nassert a defense under this section.\n\n* renumber various provisions in the interest of consistency with the\namended law.\n\nSection 2 of the bill provides that it would take effect one hundred\nand twenty days after it becomes law.\n\n EXISTING LAW :\n\nCurrently, GBL § 399-z bars any telemarketer or seller from making any\nunsolicited telemarketing sales call to any customer when that\ncustomer's telephone number has been on the national \"do-not-call\"\nregistry.\n\nThe federal Telemarketing Sales Rule (TSR) requires telemarketers to\ncall only between 8:00 a.m. and 9:00 p.m. local time and requires them\nto disclose truthfully, promptly, and clearly and conspicuously, the\n(1) identity of the seller/telemarketer; ( 2) that the purpose of the\ncall is to sell goods or services; and (3) the nature of the goods and\nservices. (16 CFR § 310.4)\n\nThe TSR, 16 CFR Part 310 et seq. prohibits certain telemarketing sales\npractices. Specifically, the TSR requires telemarketers to disclose\n\nall material terms and conditions of any offer that contains a\nnegative option feature.\n\nThe federal Telephone Consumer Protection Act of 1991 (TCPA), among\nother provisions, restricts the use of automatic dialing systems and\nprerecorded or artificial messages. One of its implementing rules, 47\nCFR § 64.200, prohibits any telemarketer from using an artificial or\nprerecorded voice to deliver a message without the prior express\nconsent of the called party.\n\n STATEMENT IN SUPPORT\n\nSince the State's Do-Not Call (DNC) law became effective in April\n2001, it has become one of the most important consumer laws enacted in\nNew York State. DNC is the most common subject of complaint received\nby the CPB's Consumer Assistance Unit. In the first two quarters of\n2008, the total number of DNC related complaints or inquiries totaled\n52,425. However, notwithstanding its popularity, the statute needs to\nbe revised to reflect new telemarketing practices and trends.\n\nTo save time and resources, many companies are turning to automatic\nRprerecorded calling as opposed to live person-to-person calling.  By\nfurther defining \"telemarketing sales call\" in the DNC statute to\ninclude any outbound telephone calling technology that delivers\npre-recorded unsolicited telemarketing sales call message either to a\ncustomer or to their voicemail or answering machine service, the CPB\nseeks to create more comprehensive protections for consumers. These\namendments are consistent with current federal TCPA protections and\nbring the State law into parity with the federal law in this regard.\n\nThis bill clarifies that prerecorded unsolicited telemarketing sales\ncalls are subject to the same enforcement provisions as \"live\" calls,\nas they are just as onerous and burdensome to New Yorkers who do not\nwant to receive unsolicited calls. Further, it is important to limit\nthe time of day that a telemarketer may call a consumer. The CPB\nenforcement staff has received numerous complaints relating to\ndisruptive late night and early morning telemarketing calls.  While\nthe federal law contains this prohibition, the current State law does\nnot. As many telemarketing calls disrupt the dinner hour, this bill\ngoes further than federal law to restrict telemarketing calls to the\nhours of 8:00 a.m. and 9:00 p.m.\n\nIn addition, the bill requires a telemarketer to provide consumers\nwith his or her name, the entity on whose behalfthe call is made, the\npurpose of the call, and the identity and cost of the goods or\nservices being offered. Such information would allow consumers to\ndetermine the nature of the call and make informed choices, and aid\nenforcement by the CPB.\n\nMany consumers have been frustrated by trial offers which invite their\nbusiness for what seems to the consumer to be for a limited time, only\nfor the consumer to realize they have signed onto a longer term\ncommitment. This bill .requires telemarketers and sellers to\ncommunicate all of the material terms and conditions of an offer when\nsuch offer contains a \"negative option\" feature, wherein the consumer\nis required to take affirmative steps to reject further provision of\ngoods or services. This provision is consistent with the TSR and\ncreates parity between State and federal law.\n\nFinally, the bill would grant CPB the power to subpoena documents and\nrecords related to possible violations of this provision. This would\ngreatly aid in enforcement of this provision, and in ensuring that\nviolators receive appropriate sanction.\n\n BUDGET IMPLICATIONS :\n\nNone.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nThis act takes effect on the one hundred twentieth day after it shall\nhave become law.\n",
